Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Author
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT - DAY

Naturalistic late-1990s live-action. A worn Kirk Street walk-up holds THE NARRATOR, late twenties, lean and watchful, and MA, a tired older woman with brown hair, a cardigan, and an afghan over her knees.

NICK, broad-shouldered and powerful, enters in big boots and a brown suede jacket. His reddish-brown hair is uncombed; old scars cut his chin and left eyebrow. His burnt-orange 1980 Trans Am, yellow flames on its sides, waits below.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Dad took off when I was about eight. Nick was the guy everybody was scared of.

NICK
What’s Ma doin’ back in the bedroom at this time of day?

THE NARRATOR
I don’t know.

NICK
You got to keep an eye on her. Old people start sleepin’ all day, and pretty soon they don’t want to go outside, or eat, or go to the store or nothing.

MA shuffles out, staring at the neat line of pill bottles on the kitchen counter.

MA
Did we go to church today? Did you see Mrs. Canasta there?

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nick is my brother. I know he’ll stand up for me no matter what.

EXT. PIZZERIA SIDEWALK - DAY

THE NARRATOR wheels his bike toward work. SIONNA, pale and slight with brown hair, light-blue eyes, a fish-symbol pendant, black V-neck shirt, jeans, and blue tennis shoes, trips on broken cement and brushes his shoulder.

THE NARRATOR
Are you okay?

SIONNA
Yes.

THE NARRATOR
I work here. What is your name?

SIONNA
Sionna.

THE NARRATOR
That’s different. What does that mean?

SIONNA
My parents named me for an Irish goddess, the matron of the River Shannon.

THE NARRATOR
Want to come in and have some pizza?

SIONNA
No thanks, I don’t eat pizza. But if you want to go have a cup of coffee sometime, you can call me.

She writes her number on a receipt. THE NARRATOR watches her walk away, unable to shake the feeling that he has known her before.

INT. MILWAUKEE PUBLIC LIBRARY - NIGHT

Under old hanging lamps and among wood floors and endless stacks, THE NARRATOR signs for a temporary library card.

He takes WUTHERING HEIGHTS from the classic literature shelf and opens it beneath the reading light.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Who needed school when you could get anything in here for free?

INT. JAVA HUT - DAY

SIONNA sits at a back table in a loose green top, backpack at her feet. THE NARRATOR joins her with two coffees.

THE NARRATOR
I’m reading Wuthering Heights. It’s about this guy and girl. They grow apart, but they’re really in love with each other.

SIONNA
The love that Heathcliff has for Catherine never changes, but in the end that kind of love destroys him.

THE NARRATOR
Strong and unwavering always seemed good to me.

SIONNA
A love that never changes will destroy you.

THE NARRATOR absorbs that. Through the window, downtown Milwaukee moves around them, suddenly larger than the life he knew.

INT. YOGA STUDIO - NIGHT

THE NARRATOR strains on a purple mat among quiet students. SIONNA moves easily beside him.

His eyes close in Savasana. A dream-image flashes: a brown mare carries him through a sunlit old forest, his arms around SIONNA in linen and a red tunic. In a grassy field, they embrace and kiss.

THE NARRATOR
I dreamed we were somewhere a long time ago. It felt like my life for real.

SIONNA
Maybe you were dreaming about something from a past life.

THE NARRATOR
What if it was?

SIONNA
Then it was beautiful.

SIONNA
Your practice is your practice. The only thing that matters is what’s going on on your mat.

THE NARRATOR
My practice is not the best practice.

SIONNA
That’s why they call it practice.

THE NARRATOR smiles despite himself. For once, he feels calm.

INT. FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT - NIGHT

MA dozes before a game show. THE NARRATOR stands ashamed beside a missed doctor’s appointment card.

NICK paces, his fists clenching and unclenching.

NICK
Ma missed her doctor’s appointment yesterday, you ass. Where the hell were you?

THE NARRATOR
I totally forgot. I’m sorry. I was with someone.

NICK
With someone? A girl is worth all this? Try looking at your priorities, Bro.

THE NARRATOR
I said I was sorry.

NICK slaps THE NARRATOR across the head. His left ear rings.

NICK
Don’t you ever talk back to me about responsibility.

NICK storms out. MA keeps staring at the television, confused by the noise and unable to follow it.

INT. JOE'S BAR AND GRILL - NIGHT

Packed bodies, smoke, pool cues, and hard rock surround NICK, THE NARRATOR, and NICK’s friends. Across the room, LINDSEY stands with a new date.

NICK
Who’s that asshole with Lindsey?

THE NARRATOR
Why does it matter? You broke up with her. She can see whoever she wants.

NICK pushes toward LINDSEY. THE NARRATOR blocks him.

THE NARRATOR
You don’t even know that guy. Leave it, man.

NICK
Keep out of it. You’re going to be next if you don’t get out of my face.

THE NARRATOR
No one cares about your ego, Nick!

NICK’s fist catches THE NARRATOR across the face. LINDSEY recoils against the wall.

LINDSEY
No! Stop!

JOE and his bouncer seize NICK. THE NARRATOR slips through the crowd and runs into the cold night.

INT. SIONNA'S LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

THE NARRATOR arrives breathless. SIONNA’s gray cat disappears down the hall as she touches his bruised cheek.

SIONNA
Your brother tried to hit you.

THE NARRATOR
He does care about me. I can’t explain Nick completely.

SIONNA
No one does that, you know. No one who really cares about you.

THE NARRATOR
I love him. I’m afraid of being alone.

SIONNA
You are becoming a bravely compassionate being. You can’t let him intimidate you, or stop you from your purpose.

THE NARRATOR
My purpose. What is my purpose?

SIONNA
To find the true self within you, and to honor it. To be the best person you can while you’re here.

Over the following months, used textbooks and a secondhand computer accumulate beside SIONNA’s couch. THE NARRATOR studies, meditates, and learns to speak without asking NICK’s permission.

INT. FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT - NIGHT

Months later. A blue weekly medication organizer sits beside MA’s chair. College books crowd THE NARRATOR’s end of the table.

NICK lays out MA’s pills with deliberate care.

THE NARRATOR
I applied for a student loan. I’m going to community college. Business, maybe. I don’t want to work in a pizza place my whole life.

NICK
Going to school? How the hell are you going to pay for that and watch Ma?

THE NARRATOR
I’ll make it work.

NICK
You are my little brother. Nothing, I mean nothing, is ever gonna come between us. Not school, not Ma, and not this girl either.

MA
I’d like to go to church again.

NICK
I’ll take you to Mass, Ma.

THE NARRATOR watches NICK kiss MA’s forehead. It looks like tenderness, but his hand grips her shoulder too tightly.

INT. JOE'S BAR AND GRILL - NIGHT

Three empty Jack-and-Coke tumblers stand before NICK. A church bulletin is trapped beneath one glass.

NICK checks his watch, grabs his keys, and limps toward the door.

EXT. WERNER AND LOWE INTERSECTION - NIGHT

NICK’s burnt-orange Trans Am carries MA home from church.

The car enters the intersection. Another vehicle slams broadside into MA’s passenger side.

Metal folds. Glass bursts. The flaming Trans Am emblem spins to a stop in the street.

INT. FRODO HOSPITAL ICU CORRIDOR - DAY

Machines beep beyond the ICU doors. THE NARRATOR stands with SIONNA as OFFICER DUPUIS, a graying sheriff’s officer, approaches.

OFFICER DUPUIS
Your brother is serious but stable. Your mother passed away at the scene.

THE NARRATOR
Nick?

OFFICER DUPUIS
His blood alcohol was high. There will be an investigation.

Through the ICU window, NICK lies unconscious beneath tubes and bandages.

THE NARRATOR
He fucking killed my mom.

SIONNA
Let it out. We’ll figure it all out.

THE NARRATOR cannot make himself enter NICK’s room.

INT. SIONNA'S LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

Weeks later. THE NARRATOR is at work. SIONNA is alone, cooking in the back kitchen.

NICK, gaunt and limping from the wreck, forces open the front door. He carries a switchblade.

SIONNA
Nick? I never expected you here.

NICK
If it wasn’t for you messing around with my brother, everything would be fine. Ma would be alive. I would be working.

SIONNA
This has nothing to do with me. Let’s talk about this rationally.

NICK pins her against the hallway wall, one hand tightening around her throat. He flicks open the blade and rests it at her left collarbone.

NICK
Get away from my brother. Get me money. I’ll be back in one week.

THE NARRATOR bursts through the front door and grabs NICK’s knife arm.

THE NARRATOR
Nick! What the fuck are you doing?

In the struggle, the blade drives into SIONNA’s upper chest. NICK elbows THE NARRATOR away and flees into the dark.

SIONNA sinks to the floor, blood spreading across her shirt. THE NARRATOR tears off his flannel and presses both hands hard over the wound.

THE NARRATOR
Sionna, stay with me. Stay with me.

Sirens grow louder. THE NARRATOR keeps pressure on the wound as SIONNA’s breathing turns shallow.

INT. SIONNA'S LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

Months after surgery, SIONNA sits in a rocking chair, her shoulder scar visible above her sleeve. A newspaper on the table reports NICK’s conviction for involuntary manslaughter and attempted murder.

THE NARRATOR kneels before her.

THE NARRATOR
Please stay. You’re safe now. I will protect you with my life.

SIONNA
I can’t feel me. I can’t feel myself anymore.

THE NARRATOR
We can work this out. See a therapist. Stay here with me.

SIONNA
I need to go to Mom and Dad’s for a while. I need to find out who I am without all of this.

She touches his face once, then walks into the bedroom to pack.

THE NARRATOR remains on the couch, staring at the empty space she leaves behind.

INT. SEATTLE APARTMENT - NIGHT

Eight years later. Rain streaks the windows. THE NARRATOR, older and steadier, returns from yoga to a warm apartment and BOZO, an orange-and-white cat.

A wall map marks twelve locations of TONY'S BOY'S PIZZA across the Northwest. THE NARRATOR sits before his laptop and opens a flight search for Milwaukee.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Holding onto resentment kept me in my own prison. I had to find forgiveness.

He books a flight to Green Bay.

INT. GREEN BAY CORRECTIONAL INSTITUTE VISITATION ROOM - DAY

Plexiglass divides THE NARRATOR from NICK. NICK is thinner now, his hair graying at the temples, his old scars lost among new lines of regret.

NICK
Hi, Bro. Glad you came.

THE NARRATOR
I need you to listen. Even though you are my brother, and we have been through a lot of shit together, I am no longer interested in seeing you or hearing anything you have to say to me.

NICK lowers his eyes.

THE NARRATOR
You killed my mother in a drunk-driving accident. You held me down my entire life. Mostly, you tried to kill the love of my life, and you drove her away.

THE NARRATOR
You’re an asshole, Nick.

NICK’s eyes fill.

THE NARRATOR
But I forgive you. I forgive you because it sets me free. It releases your control over me forever.

NICK
I’m so sorry. I am a huge asshole. I think about Ma and Sionna every day.

THE NARRATOR
I want you to get better. I still believe you can change. After all is said and done, I still love you for being my brother.

NICK
What happened with you?

THE NARRATOR
I own a pizza chain in Seattle. Tony’s Boy’s Pizza. I did it, Nick. I really did it.

NICK
I’m proud of you, man. I love you.

A guard signals that time is over. THE NARRATOR hangs up the receiver and watches NICK disappear down the prison corridor.

INT. MILWAUKEE AIRPORT - DAY

At the gate, THE NARRATOR looks down at his boarding pass: PETER OLSON.

THE NARRATOR
That’s me. President Peter Olson.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
This has been Nick and me.

FADE OUT.

THE END
